msgid "Sign in with ORCID and to a node"
msgstr ""

msgid "*Declare who you are and reach your StratiGraph node.*"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Two things are asked of whoever works online, and EMStudio keeps them "
"apart. The **author** is who signs, on this computer: the person whose "
"ORCID iD goes on the stamps, the versions and the paradata. The **node** "
"is the StratiGraph server that keeps the rooms, and your **account** on "
"it is how the node knows you. The two are tied by the same ORCID: when "
"the node's account and the author of this computer carry the same iD, "
"they are one person."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Both live in one menu: click the chip at the top right of the window (it "
"reads **Who signs?** until an author is declared). The menu opens with "
"four numbered steps, **Author**, **Node**, **Sign in** and **My space**, "
"each with a tick when it is done; a click on a step goes to its section. "
"The label at the left of the status bar (:kbd:`⇧⌘L` on the Mac, "
":kbd:`Ctrl+Shift+L` elsewhere) opens the same menu at the section **Where"
" you work**."
msgstr ""

msgid "Say who signs"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An author can be declared offline: nobody has to confirm it for you to "
"start working and saving."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The chip's menu with the Author section on top and its button Say who "
"signs, then the node and account, where you work, and my space."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Open the chip's menu. In the section **Author**, press **Say who "
"signs…**. The panel **Who you are** opens."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Under **Declare your ORCID iD**, type the iD (``0000-0000-0000-000X``) "
"and, if you wish, your name. Press **Declare**. EMStudio checks the shape"
" of the iD and its check digit: an iD with one wrong or two swapped "
"digits is refused with the reason."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The chip now shows your name, and **Author** has its tick. The identity "
"is *declared*: you can work, stamp and save. Publishing as that person "
"needs a verification."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To change the name later, or to keep several people on one field laptop, "
"use **Edit in Settings › Identity**: the **Identity** tab of "
"``Settings…`` lists the identities known on this computer, with **Use** "
"and **Forget** for each (see :doc:`/reference/settings`)."
msgstr ""

msgid "Verify the iD with ORCID"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In the section **Author** of the chip's menu, press **Verify with "
"ORCID…**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In the panel **Who you are**, the line **Verify with ORCID** says which "
"way the verification will take, once EMStudio has checked that ORCID can "
"be reached. Press **Verify with ORCID**."
msgstr ""

msgid "Desktop"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"ORCID opens in the system's browser. Sign in there; EMStudio comes back "
"by itself and reads your verified iD. If it does not come back, the page "
"shows a code: paste it in the field **Paste ORCID's answer** of the panel"
" and press **Use it**."
msgstr ""

msgid "Web"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In the web version on a node's default configuration, the verification "
"goes through the node's own ORCID sign-in: the line says **ORCID, through"
" the node**, followed by the node's name. The page goes to the sign-in "
"and comes back with the iD verified. A web page with an ORCID client of "
"its own, set in **Settings… ▸ Identity**, goes straight to ORCID instead."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The identity is now *verified*. If ORCID verifies a different iD from the"
" one you declared, nothing is changed and EMStudio says so: only you know"
" which of the two is the mistake."
msgstr ""

msgid "Choose your node"
msgstr ""

msgid "The desktop application has no node until you choose one."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In the section **Node and account** of the chip's menu, press **Choose a "
"node…**. The list **Choose the node** opens inside the menu."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"EMStudio looks in four places, and groups what it finds: **This "
"computer**, **The local network (found by themselves)**, **Saved**, and "
"**By hand**. Each node says whether it answers, its version and the ways "
"in it offers. To use a node that is not listed, type its address under "
"**By hand** (``https://…``) and press **Try**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Press **Use this node** beside the node you want. A saved node can be "
"chosen even when it does not answer now: the status bar says when it "
"answers again."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Under **This computer**, **Turn on a node on this computer** starts a "
"personal node: it listens to this computer only, and the files stay in "
"your folders. **Open to the local network…** lets the other computers of "
"the network reach it, after a question that says who will be able to come"
" in; **Close to the network** and **Turn it off** undo it."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The node is the one that serves the page, found from its address "
"(``…/em/studio/``): the step **Node** is already done. **Another node…** "
"opens the list **Choose the node**, where the saved nodes and an address "
"typed under **By hand** are checked. The search of the local network and "
"the personal node of this computer belong to the desktop application: the"
" list says that they are not available here."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The nodes this computer knows are kept in the **Connections** tab of "
"``Settings…`` (**Manage nodes and connections…** in the chip's menu opens"
" it): each one can be made the default, edited, tested and removed, and "
"**Add a node…** opens the same list to add one."
msgstr ""

msgid "Sign in to the node"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In **Node and account**, the line of the node says whether it answers and"
" how you entered it (at first **not entered**)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Press **Enter with ORCID**: the node signs you in through ORCID and "
"accredits you, so that you may publish on it. Where the node has no "
"internet, press **Enter with the node's password** instead and give your "
"iD and the password that the node's manager gave you: the node attests "
"who you are, without ORCID."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The node's sign-in page opens in the system's browser. Sign in there: "
"EMStudio comes back by itself. If it does not, copy the address the "
"browser was sent to (it starts with ``org.extendedmatrix.emstudio:/oidc-"
"return``) and paste it in the field **Paste the node's answer** of the "
"panel **Who you are**, then press **Use it**. The desktop application "
"signs in only when you ask."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The page goes to the node's sign-in and comes back to where you were, "
"with the study you had open still on it. While the node's session is "
"alive, EMStudio renews the access by itself."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Back in the menu, the node's line reads **accredited** (with ORCID) or "
"**entered as declared** (with the node's password), followed by the name "
"the node knows you by. The step **My space** gets its tick, and the "
"section **My space** lists your rooms on the node."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If the node knows you by another name than the author of this computer, "
"the menu says so in one line: in a room, what you do is signed by the "
"node's person."
msgstr ""

msgid "Sign out"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Leave this node**, in **Node and account**, forgets this session's "
"access and closes the room you are in; your identity on this computer "
"stays. **Sign in as another user** leaves the node and asks for user and "
"password again. **Sign out of this identity**, at the foot of the panel "
"**Who you are**, forgets the author on this computer; what was already "
"signed keeps its signature."
msgstr ""

msgid "What you get"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"An author who signs what you do, declared or verified, and a session on a"
" node with the access the node gave you. Creating a room, bringing a "
"study into one and publishing all need the three steps: when one is "
"missing, the gesture opens this menu at the missing step, says what it "
"needs, and goes on by itself once the step is done."
msgstr ""

